{"id":"h220","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? Johannes Kepler published Astronomia Nova in 1619.","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Astronomia Nova was published in 1609.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":3.0703},{"text":"Astronomia Nova contains Kepler's first two laws of planetary motion.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The first two laws were derived from Tycho Brahe's observations of Mars.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1244},{"text":"The year 1619 belongs to Harmonices Mundi, which contains the third law.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.9677},{"text":"Kepler's Epitome of Copernican Astronomy appeared in parts between 1618 and 1621.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1415},{"text":"Kepler published several major works within a few years of each other, making 1619 plausible-sounding.","confidence":0.7,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.0996,"phi":0.8269},{"text":"The claim is a mix-up of dates, not a wrong-author error.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":2.8321},{"text":"The publication date of Astronomia Nova is a matter of record.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1099},{"text":"The wrong year alone makes the claim false.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1368}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":5,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":8,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":5,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":5,"j":8,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":6,"j":8,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"}],"readout_weights":[0.2564,0.0513,0.0,0.205